    {
      "name": "RAG Retrieval",
      "item": [
        {
          "name": "Query — POST /v1/rag-retrieval/query",
          "description": "Real retrieval + grounded generation (MVP, as of 2026-09-10) - not a stub. A well-formed query spends one real embedding call + one real chat completion, and needs the target content already indexed (run RAG Ingestion > Upload then RAG Ingestion > Index on a real PDF first). Query decomposition, guardrails, and evaluations are still hand-written and not built. resources/golden_dataset/golden_dataset.json has 22 real question/expected-answer pairs grounded in the actual PDFs, ready to try here.",
          "item": [
            {
              "name": "Well-formed query (real grounded answer)",
              "request": {
                "method": "POST",
                "url": {
                  "raw": "{{base_url}}/v1/rag-retrieval/query",
                  "host": [
                    "{{base_url}}"
                  ],
                  "path": [
                    "v1",
                    "rag-retrieval",
                    "query"
                  ]
                },
                "header": [
                  {
                    "key": "Content-Type",
                    "value": "application/json"
                  }
                ],
                "body": {
                  "mode": "raw",
                  "raw": "{\n  \"query\": \"How many weeks of paid parental leave does JPMorgan Chase provide?\",\n  \"top_k\": 5\n}"
                },
                "description": "Expect 200, a real answer grounded in whatever's actually indexed, sources with real filename/chunk_index/score, and model_used naming the model that generated it. If nothing relevant is indexed yet, expect a fixed \"I don't have any information about that\" answer with empty sources, not an error."
              }
            },
            {
              "name": "MMR search",
              "request": {
                "method": "POST",
                "url": {
                  "raw": "{{base_url}}/v1/rag-retrieval/query",
                  "host": [
                    "{{base_url}}"
                  ],
                  "path": [
                    "v1",
                    "rag-retrieval",
                    "query"
                  ]
                },
                "header": [
                  {
                    "key": "Content-Type",
                    "value": "application/json"
                  }
                ],
                "body": {
                  "mode": "raw",
                  "raw": "{\n  \"query\": \"How many weeks of paid parental leave does JPMorgan Chase provide?\",\n  \"search_strategy\": \"mmr\"\n}"
                },
                "description": "Max Marginal Relevance - more diverse sources than plain similarity search (module 2's own rule of thumb: use this when you want relevant AND non-redundant context). search_strategy: \"mmr\" in the response; every source's score is null (LangChain's max_marginal_relevance_search() doesn't return a per-chunk score). This is the only way to select MMR now - the old dedicated /query/mmr endpoint was removed in Phase 21."
              }
            },
            {
              "name": "MultiQueryRetriever (use_multi_query)",
              "request": {
                "method": "POST",
                "url": {
                  "raw": "{{base_url}}/v1/rag-retrieval/query",
                  "host": [
                    "{{base_url}}"
                  ],
                  "path": [
                    "v1",
                    "rag-retrieval",
                    "query"
                  ]
                },
                "header": [
                  {
                    "key": "Content-Type",
                    "value": "application/json"
                  }
                ],
                "body": {
                  "mode": "raw",
                  "raw": "{\n  \"query\": \"How does 401k vesting work?\",\n  \"use_multi_query\": true,\n  \"top_k\": 5\n}"
                },
                "description": "Phase 20 - LangChain's own MultiQueryRetriever rewrites the question into several phrasings, searches with each, and merges/dedupes results - a layer on top of search_strategy, not a third option. Every source's score is null (same as MMR - MultiQueryRetriever doesn't return one). Verified live: returned 7 real chunks and a grounded answer against a real indexed 401(k) document."
              }
            },
            {
              "name": "Self-Query Retriever (use_self_query)",
              "request": {
                "method": "POST",
                "url": {
                  "raw": "{{base_url}}/v1/rag-retrieval/query",
                  "host": [
                    "{{base_url}}"
                  ],
                  "path": [
                    "v1",
                    "rag-retrieval",
                    "query"
                  ]
                },
                "header": [
                  {
                    "key": "Content-Type",
                    "value": "application/json"
                  }
                ],
                "body": {
                  "mode": "raw",
                  "raw": "{\n  \"query\": \"Show me benefits-type documents about 401k matching\",\n  \"use_self_query\": true,\n  \"top_k\": 5\n}"
                },
                "description": "Phase 20 - LangChain's own SelfQueryRetriever parses the question into a structured filter over doc_type/department/doc_classification (Phase 19's chunk metadata) before searching. is_current is never exposed to it and always stays AND-ed on regardless of what gets parsed. The response's applied_filter shows exactly what was parsed, or null if nothing was found or parsing failed (falls back to an unfiltered search, never errors). Real limitation found during live verification: doc_type is semi-controlled and filters reliably (doc_type=benefits alone matched 5 real chunks), but doc_classification is deliberately free text (Phase 19's own design), so an exact-match filter on it only succeeds when the model's guess happens to match the stored string exactly - see docs/BACKLOG.md's \"Retrieval quality\" section."
              }
            },
            {
              "name": "Edge — unknown search_strategy (422)",
              "request": {
                "method": "POST",
                "url": {
                  "raw": "{{base_url}}/v1/rag-retrieval/query",
